FILE 1, videos/l4/common.js (pure algorithms and data, no DOM). All numbers on screen must come from these functions, and L4.selfCheck() (run once at load) must throw if any lecture figure below is wrong:
 - L4.mulberry32(seed) -> () => [0,1). Reference: const mulberry32 = (a) => () => { a |= 0; a = (a + 0x6d2b79f5) | 0; let t = Math.imul(a ^ (a >>> 15), 1 | a); t = (t + Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 7), 61 | t)) ^ t; return ((t ^ (t >>> 14)) >>> 0) / 4294967296; };
 - L4.pct(p) -> "99%": integer percent, but one decimal below 1% ("0.4%", "0.1%"); L4.pctN(p) gives the rounded number only.
 - L4.ranksOf(f) -> rank per individual (1 = worst ... P = best). L4.rouletteProbs(f) -> p_i = f_i / sum f. L4.rankProbs(f, b = 1) -> {w, sum, p} with w_i = rank_i^b. L4.tournProbs(f, t) -> chance each individual wins, by the rank formula (r/P)^t - ((r-1)/P)^t (distinct fitnesses). selfCheck: roulette [2,3,5] = [.2,.3,.5]; roulette [100,.4,.3,.2,.1][0] = 100/101; rank b=1 = [5,4,3,2,1]/15; b=2 = [25,16,9,4,1]/55; b=0 = 0.2 each; tournProbs([3,8,5,1], 2) = [.1875,.4375,.3125,.0625] and t=3, t=4 as in the facts (verify against brute force over all P^t draws).
 - L4.generational(pop, kids, elites = 0) -> next population: the best `elites` individuals stay in their own slots, the other slots take kids in order (generational([.1,.5,.3,.2,.9], [.5,.3,.3,.7,.7], 0) = the kids; with 1 elite = [.5,.3,.3,.7,.9]). L4.replaceWeakest(pop, child) -> {pop, idx, looks} (looks = pop.length, idx = first minimum, replaces when child >= min); L4.replaceFirstWeaker(pop, child) -> {pop, idx, looks} (scan from slot 0, first member whose fitness is <= the child's, because the lecture counts a tie as weaker; looks = idx + 1). selfCheck: [.7,.8,.3,.9,.1] with child .5 -> weakest idx 4 / looks 5, first weaker idx 2 / looks 3.
 - L4.takeoverRows(method, seed = 4, P = 12, G = 8) with method "random" | "tournament" | "best" (reference below; counts of fitness-12 cells per row must be random [1,2,4,3,2,3,2,3], tournament [1,1,2,3,5,7,10,11], best [1,12,...]). function takeoverRows(method, seed = 4, P = 12, G = 8) {
  const r = mulberry32(seed), ri = (n) => Math.floor(r() * n);
  const base = Array.from({ length: P }, (_, i) => i + 1);
  for (let i = P - 1; i > 0; i--) { const j = ri(i + 1); [base[i], base[j]] = [base[j], base[i]]; }
  const rows = [base];
  for (let g = 1; g < G; g++) {
    const prev = rows[g - 1];
    rows.push(Array.from({ length: P }, () => {
      if (method === "random") return prev[ri(P)];
      if (method === "best") return Math.max(...prev);
      const a = ri(P), b = ri(P);               // tournament, t = 2, with replacement
      return Math.max(prev[a], prev[b]);
    }));
  }
  return rows;                                   // rows[g][i] = fitness 1..P of individual i in generation g
}
 - L4.crossMask(n, cuts) (0 until the first cut, then alternating: crossMask(8,[5]) = 00000111, crossMask(8,[2,6]) = 00111100) and L4.cross(p1, p2, mask) -> [child1, child2] (mask 1 = take the gene from the other parent). selfCheck: the three crossover results in the facts.
 - L4.spinTo(prevRot, theta, extra = 720) -> next wheel rotation (deg, clockwise) so that wheel angle theta (deg clockwise from the top, in the wheel's own frame) ends under the fixed pointer: prevRot + extra + (((360 - theta) - prevRot) mod 360, made >= 0). L4.pickSlice(f, u) -> index whose running total first passes u (u in [0, sum f)). Spins used: u = 7.3 and 3.4 on [2,3,5] (C then B, theta = 262.8 and 122.4 deg), then u = 61.0 and 12.4 on [100,.4,.3,.2,.1] (both land in the 100 slice).
 - L4.HEART (144-char bit string, row by row, 82 ones) and L4.evolveLab(kind, seed = 1, P = 30) -> {evals, snaps, at(e)}; kind "ss" = lecture algorithm 1, "gen" = lecture algorithm 2; at(e) = {evals, fit, bits} of the best individual after e evaluations (snaps are recorded each time the best improves). Reference (use exactly this, so the numbers below hold; selfCheck: evolveLab("ss",1).evals === 3069, evolveLab("gen",1).evals === 4844, both start at fit 88 after 30 evaluations; if the code is ever changed, re-check that algorithm 1 still finishes first): const HEART = "000000000000011100011100111110111110111111111111111111111111111111111111011111111110001111111100000111111000000011110000000001100000000000000000";
function evolveLab(kind, seed = 1, P = 30) {   // kind "ss" = lecture algorithm 1, "gen" = lecture algorithm 2
  const T = [...HEART].map(Number), L = 144, r = mulberry32(seed), ri = (a, b) => a + Math.floor(r() * (b - a + 1));
  const fit = (g) => g.reduce((s, b, i) => s + (b === T[i] ? 1 : 0), 0);
  const mut = (g) => g.map((b) => (r() < 1 / L ? 1 - b : b));
  let pop = Array.from({ length: P }, () => Array.from({ length: L }, () => ri(0, 1)));
  let fits = pop.map(fit), evals = P;
  const snaps = [];                              // one entry each time the best fitness improves
  const note = () => { const bi = fits.indexOf(Math.max(...fits)); if (!snaps.length || fits[bi] > snaps[snaps.length - 1].fit) snaps.push({ evals, fit: fits[bi], bits: pop[bi].join("") }); };
  note();
  while (Math.max(...fits) < L && evals < 100000) {
    if (kind === "ss") {                         // steady-state, mutation only, tournament t = 3, replace worst
      let b = ri(0, P - 1); for (let k = 1; k < 3; k++) { const c = ri(0, P - 1); if (fits[c] > fits[b]) b = c; }
      const m = mut(pop[b]), fm = fit(m); evals++;
      let w = 0; for (let i = 1; i < P; i++) if (fits[i] < fits[w]) w = i;
      if (fm >= fits[w]) { pop[w] = m; fits[w] = fm; }
    } else {                                     // generational, 1 elite, rank selection, 1-point crossover (rate 0.8), mutation
      const order = fits.map((_, i) => i).sort((a, b) => fits[b] - fits[a]);
      const rank = new Array(P); order.slice().reverse().forEach((i, k) => (rank[i] = k + 1));
      const tot = rank.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
      const pick = () => { let u = r() * tot, acc = 0; for (let i = 0; i < P; i++) { acc += rank[i]; if (u < acc) return i; } return P - 1; };
      const nx = [pop[order[0]]], nf = [fits[order[0]]];
      while (nx.length < P) {
        const a = pop[pick()], b = pop[pick()]; let c;
        if (r() < 0.8) { const k = ri(1, L - 1); c = a.slice(0, k).concat(b.slice(k)); } else c = (r() < 0.5 ? a : b).slice();
        c = mut(c); nx.push(c); nf.push(fit(c)); evals++;
      }
      pop = nx; fits = nf;
    }
    note();
  }
  const at = (e) => { let s = snaps[0]; for (const x of snaps) if (x.evals <= e) s = x; return s; };
  return { evals, snaps, at };                   // at(e) = {evals, fit, bits} of the best individual after e evaluations
}
FILE 2, videos/l4/common-2.js (drawing):
 - L4.tag(parent, {x, y, text, tone = "grey", solid = false, fs = 28}) -> {el, set({text, tone, solid, x, y, s, o})}: a compact v-tag pill: fixed height round(fs * 1.65) (46 px at 28 px, 56 px at 34 px; line-height 1, text centred, padding 0 18 px, so width = text width + 42), nowrap; o 0 hides it. All layouts below assume this 46 px height.
 - L4.tiles(parent, {x, y, vals, w = 104, h = 104, gap = 14, font = 58, tone = "grey", tones}) -> same API as L5.chromosome (el, tiles, width, height, pos(i), mid(i) -> {x, y} in stage px, set(i, {x, y, s, sx, r, o, tone, text, solid, ghost}), flip(i, k, {from, to, tone, toTone, hop}), all(fn)) but with free tile width/height and font size, so one file serves fitness tiles (font about 50 for "0.5"), letters, bits and single chips (a single tile = a chip).
 - L4.bar(parent, {x, y, len, thick = 36, dir = "h", textPos = "end"}) -> {el, set({k, tone, o, text, textTone, ghost})}: one sticker bar (grey track, rounded fill with a lip). dir "h" grows rightwards from (x, y) over len px, the value text sits in a fixed column just after the track end; dir "v" grows upwards from the baseline y, the text above the bar top. k = fill fraction 0..1 of len (a nonzero k is drawn at least 6 px so a 0.4% bar is still visible; a scene may let len stand for less than 100%, as scene 7 does), ghost = optional dashed marker at that fraction (the old value). text is whatever the scene computes (L4.pct).
 - L4.wheel(parent, {cx, cy, r}) -> {el, update({p, labels, tones, rot, hit, hitK, o})}: a roulette wheel drawn as SVG wedges, a sticker lip under it, a fixed pointer triangle above the top (orange). p = slice fractions in order (they may sum to less than 1 while slices grow in, and may be interpolated between two wheels), labels = text per slice (hidden when the slice is under 5%; a slice over 50% puts its label at 0.3 r from the centre), tones: "grey" slices alternate between two neutral shades so neighbours differ (use "red" for the superfit slice), rot = clockwise degrees, hit = index of the picked slice with hitK 0..1: it pops out 12 px along its mid-angle, gets a blue 4 px outline and (if grey) a blue fill. Slice 0 starts at the top at rot 0.
 - L4.heat(parent, {x, y, cols, rows, cw, ch, gap = 2}) -> {el, update({vals, best, shown, o})}: a framed sticker card with a cols x rows grid of rounded cells. vals = matrix [row][col] of fitness 1..max; a cell equal to `best` is orange, other cells are green with opacity 0.15 + 0.75 * (v - 1) / (best - 2) so deeper green = fitter; shown = number of rows revealed (fractional: the newest row fades and slides in).
 - L4.pixels(parent, {x, y, cell, n = 12}) -> {el, set({bits, target, tone, wrong, o})}: an n x n pixel picture, bits = string or array; ones are filled in the tone (blue, or green for the target), zeros are a pale panel colour; with wrong: true and a target given, every cell that differs from the target gets a 3 px red outline.
 - L4.knob(parent, {x, y, w, stops, thumb = 40, pillX = x - 150}) -> {el, set({v, text, o})}: a sticker slider track from x to x + w (y = its centre line) running from stops[0] to the last stop, with a numbered tick (28 px) under every stop and a round thumb at value v (fractional allowed), plus a pill at pillX showing text ("t = 2", "b = 1").
 - L4.cutLine(parent, {x, y1, y2}) -> {el, set({x, k, o})}: orange dashed vertical line, draws on from the top with k 0..1, x moves it (scene 9).
 - L4.bell(parent, {x, y, w, h}) -> {g, set({k, mark, o})}: a small Gaussian bell-curve pictogram (blue stroke), k draws it on, mark 0..1 puts a dot on the curve (scene 8).
 Scenes may add private helpers in their own file; anything needed by two scenes belongs in common-2.js.
